  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06898v2</id>
    <title>Self-Programmed Execution for Language-Model Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-10-15T15:37:58Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06898v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.06898v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>At the heart of a language model agent is an orchestrator program which is responsible for the state transition between consecutive turns. In existing systems, this program is implemented in the harness. This paper introduces self-programmed execution (SPE), in which the model completion is itself the orchestrator program, and the harness evaluates this program but does not impose any fixed orchestration policy. Formally, an SPE state of an agentic machine is one from which a model completion can load any successor state up to an embedding, and an instance of an SPE state is one which executes the model completion as a program. In practice, the program requires a language with unusual features, as its source code doubles as the content of the model's context window. I therefore introduce Spell, a Lisp dialect in which programs can edit and re-evaluate themselves, and effectful expressions like model invocations are structured such that re-evaluating an edited program does not replay its side effects. Experiments with existing models, not trained for SPE or Spell, show that frontier models can learn Spell in-context and accomplish challenging agentic tasks. These results demonstrate how a LM can act as an agent without any fixed orchestration policy, and they raise the question of what self-orchestration strategies might be learned by a model trained for self-programmed execution. Code is available at https://github.com/lukejoconnor/spell.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-05-07T19:58:28Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>Camera-ready version; accepted at NeurIPS 2026. 77 pages, including appendices and checklist</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.AI"/>
    <author>
      <name>Luke J. O'Connor</name>
    </author>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06908v1</id>
    <title>Same Signal, Opposite Meaning: Direction-Informed Adaptive Learning for LLM Agents</title>
    <updated>2026-05-07T20:15:51Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06908v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2605.06908v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Adaptive test-time compute for LLM agents aims to invoke extra computation only when it improves performance. Existing methods typically use confidence-, uncertainty-, or difficulty-based gates, assuming a fixed direction from the gating signal through compute need to the value of computation. This makes gating a utility-calibration problem: gating signals should align with whether extra computation improves the final outcome over the base policy. We show that this alignment is unstable: the same signal predicts rollout benefit in one setting and rollout harm in another, with reversals across environments and backbones even when the task is fixed. Wrong-direction gates can therefore worsen performance by precisely selecting harmful states. This reversal reflects a deeper distinction between compute need and compute suitability: a high uncertainty signal may indicate decision-difficult states where rollouts help compare alternatives, or intervention-unsuitable states where the current context does not support useful rollout-based improvement. Under this two-source model, fixed-direction gates are unreliable across heterogeneous settings. To address this, we propose DIAL (Direction-Informed Adaptive Learning), a sparse gate trained from signal-agnostic counterfactual exploration to learn the utility direction of state features per (environment, backbone). Across six environments and three backbones, DIAL yields a stronger overall success-cost trade-off than fixed-direction baselines.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-05-07T20:15:51Z</published>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.LG"/>
    <author>
      <name>Ziming Li</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Jiatan Huang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Xiaoguang Guo</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Guilin Wang</name>
    </author>
    <author>
      <name>Chuxu Zhang</name>
